WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.389 --> 00:00:07.389
Hello and welcome to this video on the Environmental Query System.

2
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:14.740
In this we're going to be doing a little bit of theory about the system and then we are going to

3
00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:22.690
be jumping in and building one inside the project. So to start off with, what is the

4
00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:31.649
Environmental Query System? Well, this is a system much like the Perception System, but

5
00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:38.049
where the Perception System is reactive to what is going on around it in the world,

6
00:00:38.609 --> 00:00:46.130
the Environmental Query System is proactive and it allows us to make more complicated

7
00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:57.299
assessments of the environment. For example, if you wanted to find a hiding spot or maybe a spot

8
00:00:57.299 --> 00:01:03.780
where you had a sniper or something that wanted a bit of cover so that it could have a good view

9
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:09.620
of shooting of things, then the Environmental Query System is what you would use. You would go

10
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:18.420
away and analyze the environment and then score it and filter them based on your tests and return

11
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:29.420
you some places which are of use to you. Now each Environmental Query goes through four stages.

12
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:35.099
So the first stage it goes through is it generates. Now you start off by generating a

13
00:01:35.099 --> 00:01:40.459
number of points that you want to check out. There's a number of different ways to generate

14
00:01:40.459 --> 00:01:46.219
these starting points. Do them in a grid, a circle, you can just grab specific actors.

15
00:01:46.859 --> 00:01:52.939
So there's a ton of different ways to generate these points. And then after that, what we do is

16
00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:58.109
we have a number of different tests that you can set up. And you can set up a whole load of

17
00:01:58.109 --> 00:02:04.829
tests and the tests will be automatically in the back end of the system. They'll be

18
00:02:04.829 --> 00:02:12.509
arranged into a sensible order. First of all, it will do any filtering that is required.

19
00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:21.310
So this is where once we've generated the points, we can eliminate points entirely based on what you

20
00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:28.349
want for your test. So for example, you could eliminate any points that were too far away or

21
00:02:28.349 --> 00:02:35.789
that had line of sight from a specific area. And after you've filtered out all of those spots,

22
00:02:35.789 --> 00:02:41.629
it will then go on to scoring the different spots. And this is where based on your tests,

23
00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:51.919
you assign a value to each spot. Now be positive or negative. But once you've done that,

24
00:02:51.919 --> 00:02:59.460
you're then going to go away and select either one spot or multiple spots. Usually,

25
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:04.639
and in our test, we're just going to be returning one spot. So for example,

26
00:03:04.639 --> 00:03:10.849
you might be returning the best hiding spot under your restrictions. And then you can either

27
00:03:10.849 --> 00:03:20.400
move to it or you can do some action based off of that. There are two core classes,

28
00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:27.280
which I would like to cover. Now we have the Environmental Query itself.

29
00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:35.680
This is the group of tests that we're looking at doing. So the generation of points and the

30
00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.199
group of tests. In our case, we're going to be finding good hiding spots, but it could be

31
00:03:41.199 --> 00:03:48.689
anything where you want to query the environment. The next one is the Environment Query Context.

32
00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:57.919
Now, in order for us to do tests, we need to provide them with context for what the

33
00:03:57.919 --> 00:04:04.340
test will be around. And there are a couple of pre-made contexts which are in the system.

34
00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:12.099
For example, the AI itself can be a context, the point itself. So if you want to test if you

35
00:04:12.099 --> 00:04:18.100
can see the point from the AI, those are the main two which are built into the system.

36
00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:23.139
If you want different contexts from this, you're going to need to write a small bit of code to

37
00:04:23.139 --> 00:04:32.019
create those contexts. Another class that we haven't touched on, and this isn't actually used

38
00:04:32.019 --> 00:04:37.220
in the game, but it's fantastic for setting up your tests and checking that they're working

39
00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:45.339
correctly, is the EQS testing war. Now this is a class that you can place into the level

40
00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:54.860
and this will, while it is selected, it will visualize all of the points that we have created

41
00:04:55.579 --> 00:05:03.040
and the results of their tests. You'll see here that they have different colours and they have

42
00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:09.680
different numbers attached to them. So first we'll talk about the blue points. Now the blue

43
00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:17.199
points are eliminated. So these are ones that are filtered out so that no tests are continually

44
00:05:17.199 --> 00:05:27.540
done on. Now you'll notice that next to each of them they have the name and a happy number.

45
00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:36.980
So you can see at this point we have distance and it's zero. So this means that these have been

46
00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:46.990
eliminated by a distance test and it was zero in the execution channel. Execution chain order

47
00:05:47.709 --> 00:05:54.110
isn't going to be the same as the order that you place the tests in because the system under

48
00:05:54.110 --> 00:06:01.629
the hood will start off with the cheaper tests. For example you could eliminate using a trace

49
00:06:01.629 --> 00:06:12.110
or a distance. However distance is considerably cheaper than trace. Therefore we will first

50
00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:19.310
run the distance check even if it is the last in the order and we will filter out those

51
00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:26.529
so we don't need to do the expensive trace test. You can cycle through this. You can change

52
00:06:26.529 --> 00:06:34.769
the number on the EQS horn so that you can see just tests one through to whatever number it is.

53
00:06:35.329 --> 00:06:41.410
I think we'll demonstrate that in the engine. However we can also see that there are a number

54
00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:51.170
of points that just have a value. Now the value will be some value it's usually between zero and

55
00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:56.560
one but it can go higher and lower depending on how you set them up. But what these are going to

56
00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:04.879
be doing is they are a scoring value. So the results of the test they will get a value from

57
00:07:04.879 --> 00:07:12.000
them. Red will indicate that you are at the low end of the gradient of the score.

58
00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:21.199
Green will be at the higher end of the score. So in this case again this is looking at distance

59
00:07:21.199 --> 00:07:30.399
so the spots that are closer to the EQS testing horn will be in green and the ones that are

60
00:07:30.399 --> 00:07:37.600
further away will be red. And this is useful because when we ask for it to select us a point

61
00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:43.759
we'll either select the best point so that has the highest value or in the top five percent

62
00:07:43.759 --> 00:07:50.829
if we want to add the level of randomness to it. So those are how these points are displayed.

63
00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:58.529
The values are the total value of the entire test. Now that brings us to the end of the

64
00:07:58.529 --> 00:08:06.079
theory for EQS. After this we will be setting one up in the project in Maxercise.
